    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before the Louisiana Purchase, a French trading post may have operated where Delaplaine now stands.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delaplaine%2C_Arkansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from French meaning 'of the plain,' originally written as De La Plaine.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delaplaine%2C_Arkansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Delaplaine returned to its current spelling in 1875, dropping the space that had separated the words.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delaplaine%2C_Arkansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town officially incorporated in 1912, establishing its municipal government and began issuing its own ordinances.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delaplaine%2C_Arkansas"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Delaplaine lies just four miles south of the Black River and two miles north of the Cache River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
